  author: "Epic Perspective News",
  title: "Myanmar Offers America a Way Into China's Rare-Earth Supply Chain. But Who Controls the Mines?",
  deck: "Myanmar's military-backed government wants American investment to challenge China's mineral dominance. The complication: its most important rare-earth mining region is controlled by an armed group fighting the government.",
  image: { src: "assets/images/epnews_myanmar_rare_earths_website_1600x900.jpg", alt: "EP News editorial graphic based on user-supplied photograph of rare-earth processing ponds", credit: "EP News editorial composite; user-provided underlying photograph; rights and precise site unverified." },
  body: [
    "MYANMAR'S INVITATION — The government in Myanmar is asking Washington to invest in its rare-earth sector, pitching the country's minerals as a way for the United States to reduce dependence on Chinese supply chains. Reuters reported the appeal on November 10, following publication of a U.S. State Department strategy that envisions deeper engagement and commercial deals.",
    "An invitation to invest, however, is not the same as control of the deposits. Myanmar's principal rare-earth mining areas in northern Kachin State are controlled by the Kachin Independence Army, an armed group fighting the military-backed government. That leaves a basic question unresolved: who could actually guarantee American companies access?",
    "WHY THE MINERALS MATTER — Heavy rare earths are important to high-performance magnets used in electric vehicles, wind turbines, advanced industrial machinery and defense applications. Myanmar supplies substantial heavy rare-earth material, much of it shipped to China for processing. China accounts for roughly 90% of global rare-earth magnet production, according to Reuters.",
    "That means gaining access to ore would not, by itself, give the United States a complete alternative supply chain. Processing, refining, magnet manufacturing, logistics and long-term commercial reliability matter too. China's existing industrial position is a major advantage.",
    "THE PROPOSED U.S. OPENING — Reuters reported that Washington's new strategy seeks at least three commercial or investment agreements worth more than $5 million each. DCI Group, a Washington lobbying firm acting for Myanmar's information ministry, argues that the United States should treat Myanmar as a critical-minerals priority and pursue a larger opportunity.",
    "Those are policy goals and lobbying arguments, not completed contracts. No evidence presented here establishes that American companies have secured access to the Kachin mines or displaced Chinese buyers.",
    "THE CIVIL-WAR COMPLICATION — Myanmar has been devastated by conflict since the 2021 military coup. The administration courting American investment does not control all of the territory containing the resources it is offering. Any proposed deal would face serious questions about security, local authority, revenue distribution, environmental safeguards and the effect on civilians.",
    "Washington also faces a diplomatic dilemma. U.S. sanctions and isolation of the military leadership after the coup were meant to respond to repression. Renewed engagement could offer leverage for negotiations, but it could also confer benefits or legitimacy on the military-backed government. Commercial aims and peace outcomes should not be treated as interchangeable.",
    "WHAT CHINA STANDS TO LOSE — If American investment eventually creates reliable alternative supply routes, Beijing could face greater competition for Myanmar's mineral output. Yet Chinese processors, intermediaries and magnet producers already hold advantages that cannot be erased by signing a mining agreement.",
    "WHO BENEFITS? — The United States could diversify access to critical minerals. Myanmar's military-backed administration could gain investment and diplomatic standing. China could face competition while retaining downstream industrial leverage. Communities and armed groups in the mining regions could experience sharply different outcomes depending on who controls the mines and who receives the revenue.",
    "THE EP NEWS QUESTION — Can Washington reduce China's grip on critical minerals without deepening Myanmar's conflict or exchanging one strategic dependency for another? A proposed deal is only the first step. The real measure of influence is whether it produces secure, lawful, sustainable supply chains and a better outcome for the people living where the minerals are extracted."
  ],
